-- Oppo Tee & Toss — Stay Inside the Ball  [Hitting]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the outer-third tee and the bottom-hand reset
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Tee and toss work aimed at the outside pitch and the opposite field. The tee goes on the outer third of the plate and slightly deeper than usual, and the hitter drives the ball into the opposite gap. Rolling over — pulling a weak ground ball — means the barrel released early, and the fix is three one-armed bottom-hand swings before returning to full ones. Four sets of eight, and it plugs straight into situational batting practice where moving a runner requires exactly this swing.',
  regression_notes = 'Move the tee back toward the middle of the plate and rebuild the path there before pushing it outside again. Outer third with a deep setup is a hard ask for a young hitter.',
  progression_notes = 'Move to front toss on the outside part of the plate, then into situational batting practice where the hitter has to choose the opposite-field swing themselves.',
  reps_guidance = '4 sets of 8 swings on the outer-third tee',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['isolate', 'game_application']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '769ab66e-93c9-41bb-8519-91bd3e0ab186';

-- Overload/Underload Bat Speed Progression  [Hitting]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the heavy-light-game sequence and the intent requirement
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Bat speed work using three bats in a fixed order: five swings with a heavier training bat, five with a lighter one, then five with the game bat. The heavy bat teaches the body to sequence under load, the light bat teaches it to move fast, and the game bat is where the two are supposed to meet. Three rounds, off a tee, with full intent on every single swing — a lazy overload swing trains slowness, which is the opposite of the point.',
  regression_notes = 'Drop the overload bat entirely and work light-bat-to-game-bat only. For younger players, swinging fast with a light bat is the whole useful half of this.',
  progression_notes = 'Progress the overload weight slowly, staying within about twenty percent of the game bat, and measure with a sensor or phone video so the change is a number rather than a feeling.',
  reps_guidance = '3 rounds: 5 heavy + 5 light + 5 game-bat swings',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['isolate', 'progress']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '40b34435-fd5f-4263-ae2f-b53083ce4531';

-- The Catch Drill — Track & Read Pitches  [Hitting]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the helmet, the glove and the scored calls
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Pitch recognition with the swing removed. The hitter stands in the box wearing a helmet, holding a glove instead of a bat, and catches the pitch — calling ''fastball'' or ''change'' as early as they can read it. Correct early calls score. Because there is no swing to get wrong, all the attention goes to the ball out of the hand, and it can be run free during a pitcher''s bullpen by rotating hitters through the box.',
  regression_notes = 'Slow the pitching down, or drop to calling ''strike'' and ''ball'' instead of pitch type. Location is easier to read than spin and still worth training.',
  progression_notes = 'Add a third pitch type, require the call earlier, or put a bat back in their hands with a take-only rule so the recognition has to survive the urge to swing.',
  reps_guidance = '2 rounds of 10 pitches, calls scored',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['isolate', 'decision']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 'feb4908a-bcde-4744-9a56-010c49b01072';

-- The Move-'Em At-Bat — Advancing Runners  [Hitting]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the situation, the zone restriction and scoring execution over outcome
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Situational batting practice for one job: a runner on second, nobody out, and the hitter has to move him to third. Set a runner at second and pitch live. Only pitches middle-away get swung at, and the ball has to go to the right side — pulling a ground ball to shortstop is a failed rep even if it finds a hole. Six scenarios a round, two rounds. Execution is scored, not outcome, because the whole point is a hitter who can give himself up on purpose.',
  regression_notes = 'Run it off front toss with the feeder only throwing middle-away, so the hitter practises the swing before also having to judge the pitch.',
  progression_notes = 'Mix in pitches the hitter must take, add a second situation such as a runner on third with one out, and let them state the plan before stepping in.',
  reps_guidance = '2 situational rounds of 6 at-bat scenarios per session',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['game_application', 'decision']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 'aeae3e6a-487e-4f74-874d-edfeb83d3446';

-- Stay Back — Beat the Changeup  [Hitting]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the varied-speed front toss and the stride-on-time rule
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'What staying back actually means, drilled against pitches that punish not doing it. A feeder throws front toss and varies fast and slow without telling the hitter which is coming. The stride still lands on time for a fastball — that part does not change — but the hands wait. Three rounds of twelve mixed-speed swings. A hitter who solves this by striding late has not learned to stay back; they have learned to be late on everything.',
  regression_notes = 'Announce the speed before each toss so the adjustment can be practised deliberately, then stop announcing. Bounce toss is a gentler introduction to an unpredictable ball path.',
  progression_notes = 'Widen the speed gap, add a third speed, or move to live pitching with a pitcher who genuinely has an off-speed pitch.',
  reps_guidance = '3 rounds of 12 mixed-speed swings',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['progress', 'decision']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '6c655fff-6181-4b7b-b22b-e1401e2e1f27';

-- Stride Pause to Stride Swing Drill  [Hitting]  → READY
-- source: written from the existing description, marker and recorded flaws (stride, timing, balance, lunging, no load)
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'The stride, separated from the swing and then put back. The hitter strides and stops — front foot down, weight still back, hands still back — and holds that position for a count of two while the coach checks it. Then, on a second call, they swing. After a set of these the pause is removed and the stride flows straight into the swing. It makes a lunging hitter feel the difference between striding and falling forward, which is nearly impossible to explain at full speed.',
  ai_coaching_notes = 'The hold is where the coaching happens. Look for the weight still on the back side when the front foot lands; if it has gone with the stride, they have lunged rather than strode. ''Land soft, stay back'' is the cue. Vary how long you wait before calling the swing so they cannot anticipate it — that is where the timing adjustment starts.',
  regression_notes = 'Hold the stride with no bat at all, or shorten the stride to a small toe tap. A hitter who cannot balance in the stride position is not going to swing from it.',
  progression_notes = 'Remove the pause so the stride flows into the swing, then add soft toss and front toss where the stride has to be timed to someone else''s release.',
  success_markers = ARRAY['Lands in a balanced stride and can hold it still before swinging', 'Weight stays back when the front foot lands', 'Stride goes toward the pitcher rather than opening out toward the dugout']::text[],
  practice_roles = ARRAY['teach', 'isolate']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 'f35c8a06-d0e9-4a8b-ac8a-efffb204f026';

-- Fun Fielding Drill for Young Players  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: rewritten from video framing; the alligator cue, soft hands and game-like format are kept from the existing description and cues
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Ground balls made into a game for players who are not yet interested in ground balls. Set two cones about ten feet apart as a gate. A coach rolls balls through the gate and the fielder has to get in front and field them, scoring a point for each clean one. Keep the rolls soft and the pace high. The mechanics being built are ordinary — glove out front, fingers down, let the ball come — but the scoring is what keeps a seven-year-old doing forty of them.',
  ai_coaching_notes = 'Keep it moving and keep score out loud; the moment there is a queue the energy is gone. ''Alligator'' — glove on the ground, throwing hand on top, jaws closing — is the cue that actually lands at this age. Roll softly enough that nobody is afraid, because a fielder managing fear is not learning hand position. Praise the ones where they got in front, not just the ones they caught.',
  regression_notes = 'Widen the gate, roll slower, and use a tennis ball. Any of the three makes it easier without changing what is being taught.',
  progression_notes = 'Narrow the gate, roll harder, alternate sides without warning, or add a throw to a target after each ball.',
  success_markers = ARRAY['Moves in front of the ball rather than reaching sideways at it', 'Glove is down with the fingers pointing at the ground before the ball arrives', 'Throwing hand covers the ball on most catches']::text[],
  practice_roles = ARRAY['teach', 'competition']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '267777ed-01d2-4985-a4aa-5a1c41299f11';

-- Four Cones Ground Ball Drill  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: rewritten from video framing; the four-cone pathway, footwork intent and existing cues are unchanged
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Four cones laid out as a pathway that forces the feet to do the work. The fielder shuffles along the cone path and a coach rolls a ground ball at each cone, so every ball is fielded from a different position and after a different movement. The cones make the approach angle and the shuffle explicit — a fielder who tries to reach sideways instead of moving their feet simply cannot get to the next ball.',
  ai_coaching_notes = 'Shuffle, do not cross over, and stay low between cones rather than standing up and resetting. ''Move through the ball'' — field it going forward, not stopped and waiting for it. Roll at a pace they can handle; this is a footwork drill and a ball they cannot reach teaches nothing. Two sets of ten is a full session''s worth.',
  regression_notes = 'Fewer cones, closer together, and roll every other pass so there is recovery time. Walking the path with no ball is a legitimate first round.',
  progression_notes = 'Widen the cone spacing, roll faster, or add a throw to a base after each ball so the rep finishes with a completed play.',
  reps_guidance = '2 sets of 10 ground balls',
  success_markers = ARRAY['Shuffles to get in front of the ball and fields through it moving forward', 'Stays low between cones rather than standing up between reps', 'Glove is out in front of the front foot on every catch']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '781605ce-10f5-400c-b7e2-6243915b5da2';

-- The Hands Routine — Infield Fielding Drill  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: rewritten from video framing; the solo wall progression, soft hands and transfer emphasis are kept from the existing description and cues
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'A solo glove routine a player can do against a wall with no partner and no field. The player throws a ball at the wall from about ten feet, fields the rebound as a ground ball, makes the transfer to the throwing hand, and throws again. Progressively: two hands, then glove hand only, then backhand, then short hops. It takes five minutes, it needs a ball and a wall, and it is the between-practices work that separates players who improve from players who wait to be coached.',
  ai_coaching_notes = 'Aim small — pick a spot on the wall, because where the ball hits determines what comes back, and a player who throws anywhere gets a random drill. ''Receive it, do not fight it'' for the soft hands. Make the transfer part of every rep: catch, grip, throwing position, then throw. Give them a rep target rather than a time, and tell them it is a daily thing rather than a practice thing.',
  regression_notes = 'Stand closer, throw softer, and stay on the two-hand stage. A tennis ball makes the short-hop stage survivable for a bare hand.',
  progression_notes = 'Move back, add short hops on purpose, or use an uneven wall so the rebound is unpredictable. The same routine works as a pre-game warm-up.',
  success_markers = ARRAY['Ball is received and funnelled rather than stabbed at', 'Transfer reaches throwing position in one motion', 'Can complete the full progression without chasing a rebound']::text[],
  practice_roles = ARRAY['warmup', 'repetition']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '192913af-c080-4361-b20e-f88f29db530f';

-- First Base Footwork & Throw Adjustments  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: rewritten from video framing; the four-quadrant throws and the glove-side-foot cue are unchanged
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Footwork around the bag for throws that are not on target, which at youth level is most of them. The first baseman sets up on the bag and a coach throws deliberately to all four quadrants — high, low, glove side, arm side — one round per quadrant. The stretch foot moves toward the throw while the anchor foot keeps contact with the edge of the bag. Six throws a round, four rounds, mixing in throws from different infield angles.',
  ai_coaching_notes = '''Chase the ball with your glove-side foot'' — the feet adjust to the throw, not the glove alone. Keep the anchor toe on the edge of the bag, not the middle, or there is nowhere left to stretch to. Throw genuinely off-line on purpose; a first baseman who only practises good throws is not being prepared for the ones they will get.',
  regression_notes = 'Throw only to one quadrant per session and keep the throws catchable. Four directions at once is too many decisions for a first-year first baseman.',
  progression_notes = 'Add short hops and genuinely wild throws, then put a runner down the line so the stretch has to be timed rather than set up.',
  reps_guidance = '4 rounds of 6 throws (one round per quadrant)',
  success_markers = ARRAY['Stretch foot mirrors the throw location automatically', 'Anchor foot stays on the bag on wide throws', 'Clean catches on throws to both sides without crossing the feet']::text[],
  practice_roles = ARRAY['isolate', 'repetition']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '6c2e8d58-e7f0-4727-b561-cb61bca27e25';

-- Double-Play Feeds & Turns  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the feed-then-turn sequence and the 4-6-3 / 6-4-3 alternation
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Double-play work for both middle infield positions, built in order. First the feeds: the fielder delivers the ball chest-high to a partner standing on the bag, from both the shortstop and second-base sides. Then the turns, with no runner: receive at the bag, clear it, and throw to first. Then both together at game speed. Alternate 4-6-3 and 6-4-3 every round so each player learns both ends of the play rather than only their own.',
  ai_coaching_notes = '''Give him the ball where his hands start'' — a feed at the chest is catchable on the move, and a feed at the ankles ends the play. Rep the turn without a runner until the footwork clears the bag naturally, because footwork learned while avoiding a slide is footwork learned wrong. Watch the exchange: one step or fewer for the pivot man.',
  regression_notes = 'Feeds only, from a standstill, with no turn and no throw to first. The feed is the half most youth teams never practise and the half that kills most double plays.',
  progression_notes = 'Add a runner at half speed, then a timed sequence from contact to the catch at first. Keep slides out of it until the footwork is automatic.',
  reps_guidance = '4 rounds of 6 turns per pairing',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['isolate', 'progress']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '971dccd3-bc0f-4043-9e2d-3417fc6f7361';

-- First Base Fundamentals — Finding the Bag & the Stretch  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the footwork-first sequence and the stretch-on-release timing
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'The starting point for playing first base: getting to the bag from fielding depth, finding it without looking down, setting the feet, and stretching to receive. Run the footwork with no ball first — sprint from fielding position to the bag, find it with the foot, square up to the thrower. Then add easy chest-high throws. Three rounds of eight, the first round footwork only. Everything harder at first base assumes this, and most youth first basemen have never been taught it.',
  regression_notes = 'Footwork only, walking rather than sprinting, until the foot finds the bag without a look. Add the ball once that is automatic.',
  progression_notes = 'Move to throws in all four quadrants, then to short hops and picks. Adding a runner down the line makes the stretch timing real.',
  reps_guidance = '3 rounds of 8 reps (footwork only, then with throws)',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['teach', 'repetition']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 'd685834e-1b6a-45ad-b06a-d7cf63447eef';

-- Daily Backhand Series  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: next steps written for the existing description, which already sets out the five-drill daily series from knees to full speed
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  regression_notes = 'Stay on the knees-only stages and roll the ball slowly. The glove angle is the skill; footwork can be added once the hand knows where to be.',
  progression_notes = 'Move through to full speed, then add a throw after each backhand so the play is completed. Balls hit rather than rolled make the read genuine.',
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '2f5901d2-ed57-4f6b-9dcc-6d1dc6110ef9';

-- Three-Ball Slow Roller Drill  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the three spaced balls and the no-reset sequence
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Three balls placed at progressively shorter distances in front of the fielder, and the fielder charges, fields and throws each one in sequence with no reset in between. It is the do-or-die slow roller — the play against a fast runner where stopping to set up loses the race. Three circuits of three balls. Because the balls come closer together, each rep is harder than the last and the fielder is already moving when they reach it.',
  regression_notes = 'Two balls instead of three, further apart, at a walk. The charging footwork can be learned slowly; the time pressure is what makes it a conditioning drill as much as a skill one.',
  progression_notes = 'Add a runner or a clock, or roll the balls rather than placing them so the fielder has to read the pace as well as charge it.',
  reps_guidance = '3 circuits of 3 balls per session',
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '2d15c6de-d4a9-414a-9755-533140dd68b0';

-- Advanced First Base — Off-Line Throws, Picks & Short Hops  [Fielding (Infield)]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the announced-then-random hop progression and the runner added last
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Academy-level first base work in three stages. Stage one: short hops thrown deliberately and announced, so the glove learns the path. Stage two: the same hops at random. Stage three: add a baserunner sprinting the line, which is what makes the stretch timing real rather than rehearsed. Three sets of eight picks, one set per stage. Also covers shifting for throws to either side and choosing when to leave the bag entirely on a wild one.',
  regression_notes = 'Announced hops only, thrown softly, with tennis balls. High-volume short-hop work bruises a glove hand quickly and a sore hand stops picking.',
  progression_notes = 'Add genuinely wild throws where leaving the bag is correct, and time the runner so the decision has a real cost attached.',
  reps_guidance = '3 sets of 8 picks (announced, random, with runner)',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['progress', 'game_application']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = '5afa81e5-ec55-4066-b62f-1b535884060b';

-- Base Running Circuit  [Baserunning]  → READY
-- source: written from the existing description and cue (team circuit, explosive first steps, efficient turns, eyes on the coach) — replaces cues copied across the category
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'A rotating team circuit with a different baserunning skill at each base. At home, an explosive three-step start on a coach''s signal. At first, a lead and a break toward second. At second, reading a coach''s point to advance or hold. At third, a read on a ball in the dirt. Players move around the circuit in small groups so everyone is working at once, and the whole thing doubles as conditioning without ever looking like conditioning.',
  ai_coaching_notes = 'The first three steps are where baserunning is won or lost, so make the start signal unpredictable at every station. ''Eyes on the coach, not on your feet'' — a runner watching the ground cannot take the extra base. Keep groups small and the rotation brisk. Timing the stations gives them something to chase and stops the circuit drifting into a walk.',
  regression_notes = 'Two stations instead of four, and remove the reads so it is pure starts and turns. Four decisions at four bases is too much for a first session.',
  progression_notes = 'Add live reads at every station, put a clock on the circuit, or run two groups against each other for a team score.',
  success_markers = ARRAY['First three steps are explosive rather than a gradual build', 'Runners look up to find the coach rather than watching their feet', 'Turns cut the inside corner of the base instead of swinging wide']::text[],
  practice_roles = ARRAY['repetition', 'team_execution']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 'ecf4c61b-0b8b-49bd-bbe5-697d42991d79';

-- Secondary Lead & Delayed Steal  [Baserunning]  → READY
-- source: description expanded into setup and execution from the existing cues, which already set out the two-shuffle timing and the break on the catcher's return throw
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  description = 'Two related pieces of baserunning that do not need speed. The secondary lead: from the primary lead, two shuffles timed so the runner''s weight lands moving toward the next base as the pitch crosses the plate. The delayed steal: taking that secondary, then breaking as the catcher throws the ball casually back to the pitcher. Ten secondary leads, then six delayed-steal repetitions. At levels where catchers lob the return throw, the delayed steal works almost every time.',
  regression_notes = 'Secondary leads only, with no steal attempt, until the timing is automatic. The shuffle timing is the foundation and the delayed steal is useless without it.',
  progression_notes = 'Add a live catcher returning the ball, then run it inside a scrimmage where the runner has to pick the moment themselves.',
  reps_guidance = '10 secondary leads + 6 delayed-steal reps',
  practice_roles = ARRAY['teach', 'game_application']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 'b9bb75c7-9ed0-47ee-9d11-2de953510027';

-- Simple Base Running Drills for Youth Practice  [Baserunning]  → READY
-- source: markers and next steps written for the existing description, which already sets out the three-base game-like setup
UPDATE public.drill_resources SET
  regression_notes = 'Use two bases instead of three and walk the routes first. The turn is the skill; the distance is just running.',
  progression_notes = 'Add reads — a coach pointing to advance or hold — and time the runs so there is a number to beat.',
  success_markers = ARRAY['Runners hit the inside corner of each base', 'First steps are hard rather than a jog into speed', 'Players look up to the coach for the next instruction instead of stopping']::text[],
  practice_roles = ARRAY['teach', 'repetition']::text[],
  updated_at = now()
